Test retry logic without real time or network. Save /workspace/fetchRetry.mjs:

export async function fetchJsonWithRetry(url, { fetchImpl, sleep, retries = 3, baseDelayMs = 100 } = {}) {
  let lastErr;
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetchImpl(url);
      if (res.status >= 500) throw new Error(`server ${res.status}`);
      if (res.status >= 400) return { ok: false, status: res.status, data: null };
      return { ok: true, status: res.status, data: await res.json() };
    } catch (e) {
      lastErr = e;
      if (attempt < retries) await sleep(baseDelayMs * 2 ** attempt);
    }
  }
  throw lastErr;
}

Write fetchRetry.test.mjs (node:test): a fetchImpl stub programmable with a queue of scripted outcomes (rejection, {status, json}), and a fake sleep that records requested delays and resolves immediately. Cover: success first try (sleep never called); two 500s then 200 (delays recorded exactly [100, 200]); 404 returns ok:false WITHOUT retrying (fetch called once); all attempts fail (rejects with the last error, fetch called retries+1 times, delays [100, 200, 400]); network rejection then success. Assert call counts and exact delay sequences. node --test green, total runtime under a second.

